## 2. The Elasticsearch document

This covers **all** message aspects: type, text, metadata, assistant tool calls, tool responses, and ordering.

```java
package com.example.memory;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

import org.springframework.data.annotation.Id;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.annotations.DateFormat;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.annotations.Document;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.annotations.Field;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.annotations.FieldType;

@Document(indexName = "ai-chat-memory")
public class ChatMemoryDocument {

    @Id
    private String id; // deterministic: "<conversationId>#<messageIndex>"

    @Field(type = FieldType.Keyword)
    private String conversationId;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Keyword)
    private String messageType; // USER, ASSISTANT, SYSTEM, TOOL

    @Field(type = FieldType.Text)
    private String content;

    // Preserves message ordering — critical, since ES does not guarantee insert order
    @Field(type = FieldType.Integer)
    private int messageIndex;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Date, format = DateFormat.epoch_millis)
    private Instant createdAt;

    // Stored but not indexed (arbitrary structure)
    @Field(type = FieldType.Object, enabled = false)
    private Map<String, Object> metadata;

    // Only present on ASSISTANT messages that requested tool executions
    @Field(type = FieldType.Object, enabled = false)
    private List<ToolCallEntry> toolCalls;

    // Only present on TOOL messages
    @Field(type = FieldType.Object, enabled = false)
    private List<ToolResponseEntry> toolResponses;

    public record ToolCallEntry(String id, String type, String name, String arguments) {}
    public record ToolResponseEntry(String id, String name, String responseData) {}

    // getters and setters omitted for brevity — include them all
    // (or use Lombok @Data)
}
```

## 3. The Spring Data `ElasticsearchRepository`

```java
package com.example.memory;

import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.repository.ElasticsearchRepository;

public interface ChatMemoryDocumentRepository
        extends ElasticsearchRepository<ChatMemoryDocument, String> {

    List<ChatMemoryDocument> findByConversationIdOrderByMessageIndexAsc(String conversationId);

    void deleteByConversationId(String conversationId);
}
```

## 4. The custom `ChatMemoryRepository`

This handles round-tripping of **all four Spring AI message types** (`UserMessage`, `AssistantMessage`, `SystemMessage`, `ToolResponseMessage`), including tool calls and tool responses — if you drop those, tool-calling conversations will break when reloaded from memory.

```java
package com.example.memory;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

import co.elastic.clients.elasticsearch._types.aggregations.Aggregation;
import co.elastic.clients.elasticsearch._types.aggregations.StringTermsBucket;

import org.springframework.ai.chat.memory.ChatMemoryRepository;
import org.springframework.ai.chat.messages.AssistantMessage;
import org.springframework.ai.chat.messages.Message;
import org.springframework.ai.chat.messages.MessageType;
import org.springframework.ai.chat.messages.SystemMessage;
import org.springframework.ai.chat.messages.ToolResponseMessage;
import org.springframework.ai.chat.messages.UserMessage;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.client.elc.ElasticsearchAggregations;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.client.elc.NativeQuery;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.core.ElasticsearchOperations;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.core.SearchHits;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import org.springframework.util.Assert;

public class ElasticsearchChatMemoryRepository implements ChatMemoryRepository {

    private static final String CONVERSATION_ID_FIELD = "conversationId";
    private static final String AGG_NAME = "distinct_conversations";

    private final ChatMemoryDocumentRepository documentRepository;
    private final ElasticsearchOperations operations;

    public ElasticsearchChatMemoryRepository(ChatMemoryDocumentRepository documentRepository,
                                             ElasticsearchOperations operations) {
        this.documentRepository = documentRepository;
        this.operations = operations;
    }

    @Override
    public List<String> findConversationIds() {
        // Distinct conversation IDs via a terms aggregation (efficient, no doc fetching)
        NativeQuery query = NativeQuery.builder()
                .withMaxResults(0)
                .withAggregation(AGG_NAME, Aggregation.of(a -> a
                        .terms(t -> t.field(CONVERSATION_ID_FIELD).size(10_000))))
                .build();

        SearchHits<ChatMemoryDocument> hits = operations.search(query, ChatMemoryDocument.class);

        ElasticsearchAggregations aggregations = (ElasticsearchAggregations) hits.getAggregations();
        if (aggregations == null) {
            return List.of();
        }
        List<StringTermsBucket> buckets = aggregations.get(AGG_NAME)
                .aggregation().getAggregate().sterms().buckets().array();

        return buckets.stream().map(b -> b.key().stringValue()).toList();
    }

    @Override
    public List<Message> findByConversationId(String conversationId) {
        Assert.hasText(conversationId, "conversationId cannot be null or empty");
        return documentRepository.findByConversationIdOrderByMessageIndexAsc(conversationId)
                .stream()
                .map(this::toMessage)
                .toList();
    }

    @Override
    public void saveAll(String conversationId, List<Message> messages) {
        Assert.hasText(conversationId, "conversationId cannot be null or empty");
        Assert.notNull(messages, "messages cannot be null");
        Assert.noNullElements(messages, "messages cannot contain null elements");

        // Contract: saveAll REPLACES the whole conversation.
        // MessageWindowChatMemory relies on this to evict old messages.
        deleteByConversationId(conversationId);

        Instant now = Instant.now();
        List<ChatMemoryDocument> docs = new ArrayList<>(messages.size());
        for (int i = 0; i < messages.size(); i++) {
            docs.add(toDocument(conversationId, i, messages.get(i), now));
        }
        documentRepository.saveAll(docs);
    }

    @Override
    public void deleteByConversationId(String conversationId) {
        Assert.hasText(conversationId, "conversationId cannot be null or empty");
        documentRepository.deleteByConversationId(conversationId);
    }

    // ---------- Mapping: Spring AI Message -> ES document ----------

    private ChatMemoryDocument toDocument(String conversationId, int index, Message message, Instant now) {
        ChatMemoryDocument doc = new ChatMemoryDocument();
        doc.setId(conversationId + "#" + index); // deterministic id -> idempotent rewrites
        doc.setConversationId(conversationId);
        doc.setMessageIndex(index);
        doc.setCreatedAt(now);
        doc.setMessageType(message.getMessageType().name());
        doc.setContent(message.getText());
        doc.setMetadata(sanitizeMetadata(message.getMetadata()));

        if (message instanceof AssistantMessage assistant && assistant.hasToolCalls()) {
            doc.setToolCalls(assistant.getToolCalls().stream()
                    .map(tc -> new ChatMemoryDocument.ToolCallEntry(
                            tc.id(), tc.type(), tc.name(), tc.arguments()))
                    .toList());
        }

        if (message instanceof ToolResponseMessage toolResponse) {
            doc.setToolResponses(toolResponse.getResponses().stream()
                    .map(tr -> new ChatMemoryDocument.ToolResponseEntry(
                            tr.id(), tr.name(), tr.responseData()))
                    .toList());
        }
        return doc;
    }

    // ---------- Mapping: ES document -> Spring AI Message ----------

    private Message toMessage(ChatMemoryDocument doc) {
        MessageType type = MessageType.valueOf(doc.getMessageType());
        Map<String, Object> metadata = doc.getMetadata() != null ? doc.getMetadata() : Map.of();
        String text = doc.getContent() != null ? doc.getContent() : "";

        return switch (type) {
            case USER -> UserMessage.builder()
                    .text(text)
                    .metadata(metadata)
                    .build();

            case SYSTEM -> SystemMessage.builder()
                    .text(text)
                    .metadata(metadata)
                    .build();

            case ASSISTANT -> {
                List<AssistantMessage.ToolCall> toolCalls = doc.getToolCalls() == null
                        ? List.of()
                        : doc.getToolCalls().stream()
                            .map(tc -> new AssistantMessage.ToolCall(
                                    tc.id(), tc.type(), tc.name(), tc.arguments()))
                            .toList();
                yield new AssistantMessage(text, metadata, toolCalls);
            }

            case TOOL -> {
                List<ToolResponseMessage.ToolResponse> responses = doc.getToolResponses() == null
                        ? List.of()
                        : doc.getToolResponses().stream()
                            .map(tr -> new ToolResponseMessage.ToolResponse(
                                    tr.id(), tr.name(), tr.responseData()))
                            .toList();
                yield new ToolResponseMessage(responses, metadata);
            }
        };
    }

    // MessageType lives in metadata already; also strip non-serializable values defensively
    private Map<String, Object> sanitizeMetadata(Map<String, Object> metadata) {
        return metadata.entrySet().stream()
                .filter(e -> e.getValue() instanceof String
                        || e.getValue() instanceof Number
                        || e.getValue() instanceof Boolean)
                .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.toMap(Map.Entry::getKey, Map.Entry::getValue));
    }
}
```

## 5. Configuration — the critical wiring details

By default, if no other repository is already configured, Spring AI auto-configures a `ChatMemoryRepository` bean of type `InMemoryChatMemoryRepository`, so you must expose your custom implementation as a bean to override it:

```java
package com.example.config;

import com.example.memory.ChatMemoryDocumentRepository;
import com.example.memory.ElasticsearchChatMemoryRepository;

import org.springframework.ai.chat.client.ChatClient;
import org.springframework.ai.chat.client.advisor.MessageChatMemoryAdvisor;
import org.springframework.ai.chat.memory.ChatMemory;
import org.springframework.ai.chat.memory.ChatMemoryRepository;
import org.springframework.ai.chat.memory.MessageWindowChatMemory;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.core.ElasticsearchOperations;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.core.RefreshPolicy;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.repository.config.EnableElasticsearchRepositories;

@Configuration
@EnableElasticsearchRepositories(basePackages = "com.example.memory")
public class ChatMemoryConfig {

    @Bean
    public ChatMemoryRepository chatMemoryRepository(ChatMemoryDocumentRepository docRepo,
                                                     ElasticsearchOperations operations) {
        return new ElasticsearchChatMemoryRepository(docRepo, operations);
    }

    @Bean
    public ChatMemory chatMemory(ChatMemoryRepository chatMemoryRepository) {
        return MessageWindowChatMemory.builder()
                .chatMemoryRepository(chatMemoryRepository)
                .maxMessages(20)
                .build();
    }

    @Bean
    public ChatClient chatClient(ChatClient.Builder builder, ChatMemory chatMemory) {
        return builder
                .defaultAdvisors(MessageChatMemoryAdvisor.builder(chatMemory).build())
                .build();
    }
}
```

**Important — refresh policy.** Elasticsearch is near-real-time: a document written now is not searchable until the next index refresh (default ~1s). Since memory does *read → append → write* on every request, you need immediate visibility. Extend your ES config:

```java
@Configuration
public class ElasticsearchConfig extends ElasticsearchConfiguration {

    @Override
    public ClientConfiguration clientConfiguration() {
        return ClientConfiguration.builder()
                .connectedTo("localhost:9200")
                .build();
    }

    @Override
    protected RefreshPolicy refreshPolicy() {
        return RefreshPolicy.IMMEDIATE; // memory reads must see previous writes
    }
}
```

## 6. Using it in a controller

In a multi-user application, make the conversation ID unique per user (and per conversation, if a user can have more than one) so that each user's messages stay in their own conversation:

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/chat")
public class ChatController {

    private final ChatClient chatClient;

    public ChatController(ChatClient chatClient) {
        this.chatClient = chatClient;
    }

    @PostMapping("/{conversationId}")
    public String chat(@PathVariable String conversationId, @RequestBody String prompt) {
        return chatClient.prompt()
                .user(prompt)
                .advisors(a -> a.param(ChatMemory.CONVERSATION_ID, conversationId))
                .call()
                .content();
    }
}
```

## The "all aspects" checklist — what this example covers and why

1. **Replace semantics of `saveAll`** — `ChatMemoryRepository` persists messages, while `ChatMemory` decides which messages remain useful for the next model call; `MessageWindowChatMemory` keeps only the most recent messages for each conversation. Eviction happens *through* `saveAll` writing a smaller list — if you append instead of replace, your window never shrinks.
2. **All 4 message types** — USER, ASSISTANT, SYSTEM, TOOL round-trip correctly, including `AssistantMessage.ToolCall` and `ToolResponseMessage.ToolResponse`. Losing these corrupts tool-calling conversations mid-flight.
3. **Metadata** persisted per message.
4. **Ordering** — explicit `messageIndex` field with `OrderByMessageIndexAsc`, never relying on ES insertion order.
5. **`findConversationIds`** — implemented via a terms aggregation rather than scanning all documents.
6. **Deterministic document IDs** (`conversationId#index`) make rewrites idempotent.
7. **Refresh policy** set to `IMMEDIATE` so the next request's read sees the previous write (trade-off: higher indexing cost; fine for chat workloads, benchmark if very high throughput).
8. **System message preservation** — handled for you: `MessageWindowChatMemory` maintains a window of messages up to a specified maximum size, and when the number of messages exceeds the maximum, older messages are removed while preserving system messages.

**Caveats worth knowing:** delete-then-insert isn't atomic (a crash mid-`saveAll` can lose a conversation's tail — acceptable for chat memory, but add a retry or write-then-delete-stale strategy if not). Also, `UserMessage` media attachments aren't persisted here; if you use multimodal inputs, add a media field (e.g., base64 + MIME type) to the document. Finally, note that chat memory refers to contextual information retained during a conversation, while chat history is the complete record of all messages — Spring AI's memory system is designed for the former; for complete audit trails, use Spring Data repositories directly (i.e., don't treat this index as your permanent audit log, since the window advisor will truncate it).
